The proof is outlined in \cite{krishnamurthy2021langevin} at the bottom of Page 10. Recall \cite{karatzas1991brownian} that for a generic diffusion process denoted by $dx(t) = f(x)dt + \sigma(x)dW(t)$, the stationary distribution $p$ satisfies 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:statsuff}
    \mathcal{L}^*p = \frac{1}{2}\textrm{Tr}[\nabla^2(\Sigma p)] - \textrm{div}(fp) = 0, \quad \textrm{where } \Sigma = \sigma\sigma'
\end{equation}
and $\mathcal{L}^*$ is the forward operator. From \eqref{eq:stram_diff}, $f(\alpha) = [-\frac{\beta}{2}\pi(\alpha)\nabla_{\alpha}J(\alpha) + \nabla_{\alpha}\pi(\alpha)]\pi(\alpha), \, \sigma = \pi(\alpha)I$.  Then, with $p(\alpha) \propto \exp(-\beta J(\alpha))$, 
\[\frac{1}{2}\textrm{Tr}[\nabla^2(\Sigma p)] = \sum_i\nabla\frac{1}{P}[ -\pi^2(\alpha)\frac{\beta}{2}\exp(-\beta J(\alpha))(\nabla_{\alpha}J(\alpha))_i + \pi(\alpha)(\nabla_{\alpha}\pi(\alpha))_i\exp(-\beta J(\alpha))] = \textrm{div}(fp)\]
where $P$ is the normalizing factor for $p(\alpha)$ and $(x)_i$ refers to the $i'th$ component of vector $x\in\mathbb{R}^{N}$.

Recall $\pi_{\infty}(w) := \frac{1}{\Lambda} \exp(-\beta J(w))$, where $\Lambda = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\exp(-\beta J(w))dw$. Since $\pi_{\infty} > 0$ everywhere, we can write

\begin{align*}
D(\pi_{0,\gamma} || \pi_{\infty}) &= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\pi_{0,\gamma}(x) \log\left( \frac{\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)}{\pi_{\infty}(x)}\right)dx \\
& = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)\log\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)dx + \log\Lambda + \beta\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)J(x) dx \\
& \leq \log\|\pi_{0,\gamma}\|_{\infty} + \log\Lambda + \beta\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)J(x) dx \\
& \leq \log\bar{\pi}_{0,\gamma} + \log\Lambda + \beta\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\pi_{0,\gamma}(x)J(x) dx
\end{align*}
First let us upper bound the normalization constant:
\begin{align*}
\Lambda &= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}e^{-\beta J(x)}dx \\
